Cold review of d13147e3 (S2a-1) found this; it is a measurement-validity finding. SemanticProjection.canonical is invariant under renaming ids and permuting storage. But CompareCore still breaks ties by view order: the head tie at compat.scala ~236 and the greedy relation tie at ~290. On canonical graphs, view order is the canonical labelling, which is:
(a) SHA-256 colour order of content: renaming a non-matching lemma (cat to feline) flips argumentMatch 0.65 to 0.9 on the suite's greedyA/greedyB [simulated by the reviewer; to be run];
(b) sensitive to focus and to an explicit-Unknown vs absent polarity entry, neither of which SemanticGraph holds.
ADR 0019 rejects a content-hash tie-break as a hidden prior.
Acceptance:
- the strict (canonical) compare resolves head and relation ties order-free, e.g. by an optimal assignment or by the max over tied choices, stated in the ADR;
- a law: canonical compare is invariant under focus changes, under explicit-Unknown polarity, and under renaming a lemma that does not affect any concept score;
- historical ChartCompatibility output is unchanged (S2a-0 pins).
Also: the gate-flag claim in d13147e3 and Fray #93 seq 799 is false in general. The reviewer's hand counterexample: a head tie across pb1 (polarity gate) and pb2 (reversal gate), where the ids pick which flag is set. Run it and pin it as the teeth control.
